---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Process"
  description="How a Signalworks project runs, start to finish: audit, fixed quote, build, review on a live link, launch, handoff. All remote, all in plain English."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Process</span>
      <h1>You should know exactly what happens next.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
        Most people have hired a web person once and regretted it. Vague
        timelines, invoices that grow, a site they can't touch afterward.
        Here's the whole process, so you can hold me to it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>The first conversation</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            You send a few lines about your business through the contact
            page, or we get on a short call. I'll ask what jobs you want more
            of, who calls you now, and what's annoying you about the current
            site. If I'm not the right fit — wrong project type, wrong
            budget, wrong timing — I'll say so in that first exchange, not
            after a "discovery phase."
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The video audit</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            If you have an existing site, I record a screen-share video
            walking through it: what's working, what's costing you calls, and
            what I'd change. You watch it on your own time. No meeting
            required, nothing to prepare.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The quote</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            You get a written scope — pages, features, what's included, what
            isn't — with a flat price and a delivery date. It's a short
            document in plain English. If anything in it is unclear, that's
            my fault; ask and I'll rewrite it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Kickoff</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Half the fee books your slot. Then one working session, on video:
            you talk about your business, I take notes and ask questions.
            That conversation becomes your website's copy — in your words,
            tightened up. No forty-question homework packet.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The build, on a live link</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Within days you get a private preview link to the real site — not
            mockups, not wireframes. You click through it on your own phone,
            send notes by email or voice memo whenever suits you, and watch
            revisions land. We iterate until it sounds like you.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Launch</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            The site goes live on your domain, in accounts you own. I handle
            the technical moving parts — DNS, redirects from the old site,
            search engine sitemap — and stay on watch the first week for
            anything odd. The remaining balance is due at launch, after
            you've signed off.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Handoff</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            You get a recorded walkthrough of your site, a plain-English
            owner's guide, and every login. From there you can run it
            yourself, email me for one-off edits, or take the monthly care
            plan. All three are fine. The point is it's yours.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Working remotely</span>
        <h2>Remote isn't a compromise. It's the format.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Video audits you watch on your schedule. Calls when talking is
          faster. Async review links for everything else. You never drive
          anywhere, and nothing waits for a meeting.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Video audits</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Recorded screen-shares you can rewatch and forward to a partner.
            Clearer than a slide deck, cheaper than a lunch meeting.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Live calls</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For kickoff and anything genuinely faster out loud. Scheduled
            around your jobs, not my calendar.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Async review</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Preview links and email threads you answer at 9pm from the couch.
            Most clients never need more than this.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Commitments</span>
        <h2>What I hold myself to.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Not outcomes — conduct. These are the terms of working with me,
          stated up front so you can quote them back at me later.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Replies within one business day. Usually much faster.</li>
        <li>Fixed quotes in writing. The number doesn't move inside the agreed scope.</li>
        <li>A delivery date in the quote, treated as part of the deal.</li>
        <li>You own everything: domain, hosting, code, and every account, from day one.</li>
        <li>Plain English in every document, invoice, and explanation. If I can't explain a decision simply, I'll rework it until I can.</li>
        <li>If I'm the wrong fit, I say so at the start — and where I can, point you to a better option.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Start with step one."
    body="A few plain sentences about your business is enough. The process takes it from there."
  />
</Base>
